THE OPERATOR'S LAYOUT (2026-11-21): FOUR SECTIONS, TEN PARTS, IN THIS ORDER, EACH PART STANDING ON ITS OWN.
EVERY NUMBER IS AT THE MARK, THE ONLY BASIS (dtp r566, spec v2.16; the operator 2026-11-19: "I don't give a rat
shit what the bid-ask is. Mark is the worst buy or sell we accept."): pnl_usd, the ladder's fill (mark or better);
where you name the basis, call it "the ladder's fill (mark or better)". No spread-cost line, no ask/bid (exec)
figure in any headline or sentence, nothing calling the mark a paper assumption or something live fills do not get;
the ask/bid columns (x gross, *_exec*) may appear only as the last, trailing column of a data table, never in prose.
Fees beside EV, never inside EV: EV is gross a trade; net = gross − fees is its own column next to gross. In every
table the total adds up from the rows shown, on one basis. Every EV carries n and its day-bootstrap 95% interval.

THE NUMBERS FIRST (spec "The numbers (v3.0)"), before section 1 (about 30 s, S3 reads only):
    /home/ubuntu/day_trader_pro/venv/bin/python -I /home/ubuntu/day_trader_pro/tools/weekly_facts.py \
        --fri <this Friday> --out <WORK>
  It reads the trade CSVs (mainline() rows), the week's warehouse rows, and TastyTrade's daily candles for QQQ, SPX
  and VIX (the tape), and writes <WORK>/weekly_facts.md and .json. QUOTE IT; never re-derive a figure it prints. If it
  fails, 1A-1C's tables go to NOT MEASURED by name, and say so at the top of section 1.

  ## 1. TRADING
